CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ constrained partial specialization selected by requires-clause: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*is unknown.*$
--
C++20 constrained partial specialization selection ([temp.class.spec.match]/1-2,
[temp.constr.order]): when several class-template partial specializations share
the same template-argument pattern and differ only by their requires-clauses,
the most-constrained satisfied one is selected.

This is the minimal model of libstdc++'s std::__iterator_traits<_Iterator, void>
specializations (distinguished by __iter_with_nested_types etc.).  Previously the
front end kept whichever same-pattern specialization was matched first, ignoring
the requires-clause; it now follows ID_specialization_of back to the primary and
re-runs the constraint-aware selection for requires-clause-constrained
specializations too.
